```
Reason:
The bug occurs because headroom expansion via `pskb_expand_head()` is only attempted when `hdr->segments_left == 0`. However, the recompressed SRH (via `ipv6_rpl_srh_compress()`) can grow larger than the original header on any segment swap (e.g., when `CmprI=0, CmprE>0, seg[0][0] != daddr[0]` adds up to 8 bytes). On earlier segments (`segments_left > 0`), no expansion is done, so the subsequent `skb_push()` may consume the last remaining headroom. After the push and `skb_reset_network_header()`, the call to `skb_mac_header_rebuild(skb)` writes `mac_len` bytes into the space before the new network header, but if the available headroom is less than `mac_len`, the write occurs past `skb->head`, causing a buffer overflow.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
chdr_len = sizeof(struct ipv6hdr) + ((chdr->hdrlen + 1) << 3);
if (unlikely(!hdr->segments_left ||
             skb_headroom(skb) < chdr_len + skb->mac_len)) {
    if (pskb_expand_head(skb, chdr_len + skb->mac_len, 0, GFP_ATOMIC)) {
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that headroom expansion is performed whenever the current headroom is insufficient to accommodate the sum of the new header size (`chdr_len`) plus `skb->mac_len`. This condition is now checked even when `segments_left > 0`. Additionally, the expansion size is extended by `skb->mac_len` to guarantee that after `skb_push(chdr_len)` and `skb_reset_network_header()`, the MAC header rebuild (`skb_mac_header_rebuild()`) has enough room to write the MAC header without overflowing the buffer.

